# Allow Windows-testbench scripts and docs, but never its build output.
#
# This used to force-include *.exe and *.dll, so three compiled binaries were
# committed to a public repo. Nothing used them: every wrapper script builds
# from source and runs out of target/release, and the README already said the
# committed copies were stale. Unverifiable executables in the repo of an app
# that installs a network driver are exactly what makes people distrust it.
!Windows-testbench/
!Windows-testbench/*.ps1
!Windows-testbench/*.md
cargo-core.log


# Fetched by the release workflow (SHA-256 verified there), never committed.
# Local build copies would go stale and ship unverifiable binaries.
swifttunnel-desktop/src-tauri/resources/drivers/winpkfilter/
swifttunnel-desktop/src-tauri/resources/tools/goodbyedpi/
swifttunnel-desktop/src-tauri/resources/tools/nvidiaProfileInspector/Reference.xml
swifttunnel-desktop/src-tauri/resources/tools/nvidiaProfileInspector/nvidiaProfileInspector.exe.config
swifttunnel-desktop/src-tauri/resources/tools/nvidiaProfileInspector/nvidiaProfileInspector.pdb

swifttunnel-lite/target-msi/
